			<div class="content" id="post-content-4824943">
									

<p>Upon retrospect, I think there was a fair amount of misinterpretation involved with the original post. I'd like to take a moment to reaffirm your stance, and what you hope to achieve with this article.</p>
<p>I'm visualizing two different interpretations in my head, both formed from your reply and the content of original post. You wanted to make this a comedy, which stuck to me as problematic. If you wanted to tell an anti-harassment story involving the objectification of women, making it comedy based spoke as an intense clashing of themes, and a desire to make this a &quot;haha boobs&quot; joke, which is where my original dissent came from.</p>
<p>If this is to be a protest against the type of behavior outlined in the original post, then that's completely different. As I said, how much <em>comedy</em> is it that you want, or indeed, how much is healthy for this type of story? My question stands as not what frivolous changes might make this work, but rather the bigger picture.</p>
<p>As an aside, 1004 was written in 2011, back when site standards and ediquette were different. I don't want you to just ask yourself &quot;is this okay to write?&quot;, I want you to ask yourself &quot;am I able to write this in a respectful, sensible manner while still conveying the story I wish to tell?&quot;</p>

							</div>

								
					
